1
00:00:00,088 --> 00:00:02,212
A man who has advised every White House

2
00:00:02,225 --> 00:00:04,175
since nine eleven says we are entering the

3
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:06,525
eight most dangerous weeks of our lives.

4
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:09,075
Three crises are colliding at once:

5
00:00:09,488 --> 00:00:11,675
a tense transfer of power in America,

6
00:00:12,088 --> 00:00:14,087
a three day bombing plan for Iran,

7
00:00:14,462 --> 00:00:15,862
and a dark winter for Ukraine.

8
00:00:16,288 --> 00:00:18,375
Professor Robert Pape laid it all out on

9
00:00:18,388 --> 00:00:19,825
The Diary of a CEO.

10
00:00:20,238 --> 00:00:22,812
This is that ninety minute conversation in ten

11
00:00:22,825 --> 00:00:23,312
minutes.

12
00:00:23,725 --> 00:00:25,388
How the chain could lead to world war

13
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:27,900
three, and the three firebreaks he says can

14
00:00:27,912 --> 00:00:28,688
still stop it.

15
00:00:29,179 --> 00:00:29,529
Part one.

16
00:00:29,942 --> 00:00:31,567
The eight most dangerous weeks.

17
00:00:31,979 --> 00:00:34,629
Robert Pape has studied political violence around the

18
00:00:34,642 --> 00:00:36,017
world for thirty years.

19
00:00:36,442 --> 00:00:37,204
The last five,

20
00:00:37,554 --> 00:00:39,792
he says, he has spent on one country:

21
00:00:40,092 --> 00:00:41,042
the United States.

22
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:43,617
His starting point is the midterm elections.

23
00:00:44,029 --> 00:00:46,717
Americans elect a president every four years,

24
00:00:46,917 --> 00:00:48,304
but Congress every two.

25
00:00:48,717 --> 00:00:50,054
When the vote falls in the middle of

26
00:00:50,067 --> 00:00:50,979
a president's term,

27
00:00:51,367 --> 00:00:52,204
it's called a midterm.

28
00:00:52,617 --> 00:00:54,154
Right now, Pape notes,

29
00:00:54,342 --> 00:00:56,942
the president's party controls both the House and

30
00:00:56,954 --> 00:00:59,304
the Senate, which means very little check on

31
00:00:59,317 --> 00:00:59,892
the president.

32
00:01:00,304 --> 00:01:02,242
Lose them, and that changes overnight.

33
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:04,592
But Pape's real worry isn't the vote.

34
00:01:05,004 --> 00:01:05,917
It's what comes after.

35
00:01:06,329 --> 00:01:09,354
Counting, certifying, and seating the winners takes about

36
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:10,104
two months.

37
00:01:10,517 --> 00:01:11,292
And in his view,

38
00:01:11,592 --> 00:01:13,767
the transfer of power is the danger zone

39
00:01:13,779 --> 00:01:15,067
for political violence.

40
00:01:15,492 --> 00:01:17,142
We saw it on January sixth,

41
00:01:17,317 --> 00:01:18,517
which, he points out,

42
00:01:18,779 --> 00:01:20,417
came at the end of exactly this kind

43
00:01:20,429 --> 00:01:21,679
of two month process.

44
00:01:22,104 --> 00:01:23,604
Then he shows the number that keeps him

45
00:01:23,617 --> 00:01:24,229
up at night.

46
00:01:24,642 --> 00:01:26,254
In his latest national survey,

47
00:01:26,554 --> 00:01:29,604
over forty million Americans support using the military

48
00:01:29,617 --> 00:01:32,179
against protesters who oppose the Trump agenda.

49
00:01:32,592 --> 00:01:35,167
And over sixty million support using force to

50
00:01:35,179 --> 00:01:36,867
stop Trump from being president.

51
00:01:37,279 --> 00:01:39,079
Violence on both sides of the aisle.

52
00:01:39,504 --> 00:01:42,404
Pape says these numbers have doubled since September

53
00:01:42,417 --> 00:01:43,292
twenty twenty four.

54
00:01:43,704 --> 00:01:44,892
And what if Democrats win?

55
00:01:45,317 --> 00:01:45,867
Subpoenas.

56
00:01:46,279 --> 00:01:48,592
Impeachments, and not just of the president,

57
00:01:48,879 --> 00:01:50,392
but of cabinet secretaries.

58
00:01:50,804 --> 00:01:51,679
In Pape's words,

59
00:01:51,892 --> 00:01:53,542
there is no political bottom there.

60
00:01:53,967 --> 00:01:54,692
But here's the thing.

61
00:01:55,117 --> 00:01:56,692
This isn't happening in isolation.

62
00:01:57,104 --> 00:01:58,754
It's happening in the middle of a war

63
00:01:58,767 --> 00:01:59,342
with Iran.

64
00:01:59,754 --> 00:02:00,654
And at the same time,

65
00:02:01,029 --> 00:02:03,017
Russia is threatening Ukraine's power grid.

66
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:05,554
Three crises, the same eight weeks.

67
00:02:05,979 --> 00:02:07,467
To see why they might feed each other,

68
00:02:07,879 --> 00:02:09,954
you first need Pape's most important idea.

69
00:02:10,446 --> 00:02:10,883
Part two.

70
00:02:11,308 --> 00:02:12,346
The escalation trap.

71
00:02:12,758 --> 00:02:13,921
An escalation trap,

72
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:16,921
Pape explains, is when a strong country attacks

73
00:02:16,933 --> 00:02:18,871
a weaker one and wins tactically.

74
00:02:19,296 --> 00:02:20,583
The bombs hit their targets.

75
00:02:20,996 --> 00:02:21,746
Leaders are killed.

76
00:02:22,158 --> 00:02:24,058
And yet the weaker country gains power.

77
00:02:24,471 --> 00:02:25,158
In this war,

78
00:02:25,246 --> 00:02:27,983
he says, Iran quickly tightened its grip on

79
00:02:27,996 --> 00:02:29,071
the Strait of Hormuz,

80
00:02:29,321 --> 00:02:29,883
and with it,

81
00:02:30,271 --> 00:02:32,208
about twenty percent of the world's oil.

82
00:02:32,621 --> 00:02:33,196
Most people,

83
00:02:33,558 --> 00:02:36,058
Pape says, think of strategy as chess.

84
00:02:36,471 --> 00:02:37,383
White takes a pawn,

85
00:02:37,808 --> 00:02:39,858
looks ahead, then loses a bishop.

86
00:02:40,271 --> 00:02:42,696
In chess, the sensible move is to offer

87
00:02:42,708 --> 00:02:43,108
a draw.

88
00:02:43,533 --> 00:02:45,358
That's what everyone keeps telling the president:

89
00:02:45,758 --> 00:02:46,458
just take a deal.

90
00:02:46,883 --> 00:02:49,533
But Pape says international politics isn't chess.

91
00:02:49,946 --> 00:02:50,458
It's roulette.

92
00:02:50,871 --> 00:02:51,533
You bet on red.

93
00:02:51,946 --> 00:02:52,796
It comes up black.

94
00:02:53,208 --> 00:02:54,708
A rational player walks away.

95
00:02:55,133 --> 00:02:56,396
But the gambler doubles down.

96
00:02:56,821 --> 00:02:58,121
Since red didn't come up,

97
00:02:58,408 --> 00:02:59,483
surely it will next time.

98
00:02:59,896 --> 00:03:00,971
It comes up black again.

99
00:03:01,383 --> 00:03:02,333
Now he's way behind,

100
00:03:02,708 --> 00:03:04,671
so he borrows chips from a bookie and

101
00:03:04,683 --> 00:03:05,583
bets even bigger.

102
00:03:06,008 --> 00:03:08,146
Pape maps each spin onto the war.

103
00:03:08,571 --> 00:03:09,008
Bet one:

104
00:03:09,433 --> 00:03:10,558
kill the Supreme Leader,

105
00:03:10,858 --> 00:03:11,771
and Iran will weaken.

106
00:03:12,183 --> 00:03:13,296
It didn't, he says.

107
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:15,871
Bet two: a six week air campaign.

108
00:03:16,283 --> 00:03:17,383
Iran kept the strait.

109
00:03:17,808 --> 00:03:19,196
Bet three: a blockade.

110
00:03:19,621 --> 00:03:20,421
Six months later,

111
00:03:20,783 --> 00:03:23,671
Iran hasn't collapsed and gas prices keep rising.

112
00:03:24,083 --> 00:03:24,696
And the chips?

113
00:03:25,108 --> 00:03:26,421
American gas prices,

114
00:03:26,621 --> 00:03:29,033
American lives, American interest rates.

115
00:03:29,458 --> 00:03:31,358
The borrowed chips are the party's support.

116
00:03:31,771 --> 00:03:33,733
And the bookie wants them back on January

117
00:03:33,746 --> 00:03:35,908
third, when the new Congress is seated.

118
00:03:36,333 --> 00:03:37,233
So why not just stop?

119
00:03:37,646 --> 00:03:38,983
Because, Pape says,

120
00:03:39,158 --> 00:03:41,308
presidents don't walk away from their legacy.

121
00:03:41,721 --> 00:03:44,246
Lyndon Johnson played the same game in Vietnam,

122
00:03:44,621 --> 00:03:47,108
chasing his losses up the escalation ladder.

123
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:49,183
That pressure of humiliation,

124
00:03:49,571 --> 00:03:52,171
he warns, pushes the loser to keep doubling

125
00:03:52,183 --> 00:03:52,546
down.

126
00:03:52,971 --> 00:03:54,358
Which brings us to the plan on the

127
00:03:54,371 --> 00:03:55,346
president's desk.

128
00:03:55,846 --> 00:03:56,358
Part three.

129
00:03:56,783 --> 00:03:57,621
The three day plan.

130
00:03:58,046 --> 00:03:58,908
According to Pape,

131
00:03:59,208 --> 00:04:00,696
there is a plan to hit targets in

132
00:04:00,708 --> 00:04:02,208
Iran over three days.

133
00:04:02,621 --> 00:04:04,696
Reports say the military has been told to

134
00:04:04,708 --> 00:04:07,296
be ready to execute within ninety six hours

135
00:04:07,308 --> 00:04:08,058
of a go order.

136
00:04:08,471 --> 00:04:10,721
And embassy families have already been pulled out

137
00:04:10,733 --> 00:04:11,996
of several Gulf states.

138
00:04:12,421 --> 00:04:12,946
The media,

139
00:04:13,296 --> 00:04:15,946
he says, heard three days and assumed it

140
00:04:15,958 --> 00:04:16,496
was minor.

141
00:04:16,921 --> 00:04:17,721
Pape disagrees.

142
00:04:18,146 --> 00:04:19,171
The leaked targets,

143
00:04:19,333 --> 00:04:21,746
leadership, refineries, bridges,

144
00:04:21,958 --> 00:04:22,408
energy,

145
00:04:22,808 --> 00:04:25,021
look to him exactly like Instant Thunder,

146
00:04:25,321 --> 00:04:27,271
the plan that opened the nineteen ninety one

147
00:04:27,283 --> 00:04:28,333
war against Iraq.

148
00:04:28,758 --> 00:04:30,558
It was also meant to last three days.

149
00:04:30,983 --> 00:04:32,283
It went on for forty three,

150
00:04:32,571 --> 00:04:34,608
and never paralyzed Iraq's military.

151
00:04:35,021 --> 00:04:36,083
And here's the paradox.

152
00:04:36,508 --> 00:04:38,096
The whole point of a plan like this

153
00:04:38,208 --> 00:04:39,621
is to paralyze the government.

154
00:04:40,046 --> 00:04:40,983
But if you succeed,

155
00:04:41,346 --> 00:04:42,871
who is left to negotiate with?

156
00:04:43,283 --> 00:04:44,846
And if you don't fully succeed,

157
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:47,896
Pape expects the remaining forces to lash back

158
00:04:47,908 --> 00:04:48,508
on their own,

159
00:04:48,883 --> 00:04:51,196
at airports and infrastructure across the region.

160
00:04:51,621 --> 00:04:53,908
He points out that Iran's allies in Yemen,

161
00:04:54,183 --> 00:04:57,671
the Houthis, have already struck Riyadh's international airport,

162
00:04:57,971 --> 00:05:00,421
which he calls the first intentional targeting of

163
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:01,683
civilians in this war.

164
00:05:02,108 --> 00:05:03,608
Then there are civilians in Iran.

165
00:05:04,033 --> 00:05:06,983
Pape estimates the nineteen ninety one campaign killed

166
00:05:06,996 --> 00:05:09,808
between five and fifteen thousand civilians.

167
00:05:10,221 --> 00:05:11,171
And deaths like that,

168
00:05:11,546 --> 00:05:13,883
he warns, lead to something harder to stop:

169
00:05:14,283 --> 00:05:15,358
inspired attacks.

170
00:05:15,771 --> 00:05:18,483
An inspired attack isn't ordered by any government.

171
00:05:18,896 --> 00:05:20,858
It's an individual acting on his own,

172
00:05:21,183 --> 00:05:22,958
fueled by calls for revenge.

173
00:05:23,383 --> 00:05:26,496
Pape, who has studied eight thousand suicide attacks,

174
00:05:26,721 --> 00:05:28,746
points to a flight from Dubai to Tel

175
00:05:28,758 --> 00:05:31,271
Aviv, where the co pilot attacked the pilot

176
00:05:31,283 --> 00:05:33,533
with a crash axe just thirty minutes from

177
00:05:33,546 --> 00:05:33,946
landing.

178
00:05:34,358 --> 00:05:36,208
A passenger who happened to be a pilot

179
00:05:36,221 --> 00:05:36,958
landed the plane.

180
00:05:37,383 --> 00:05:38,321
Pape can't be certain,

181
00:05:38,633 --> 00:05:39,658
but based on the timing,

182
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:41,096
he puts the odds that it was a

183
00:05:41,108 --> 00:05:43,921
planned suicide attack at around ninety percent.

184
00:05:44,412 --> 00:05:44,938
Part four.

185
00:05:45,362 --> 00:05:46,312
Putin's next move,

186
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:47,725
and a wildfire at home.

187
00:05:48,138 --> 00:05:49,625
Now, the second crisis.

188
00:05:50,038 --> 00:05:51,325
What does Putin actually want?

189
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:52,788
Pape pulls up the map.

190
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:55,950
Russia has formally annexed four regions of Ukraine,

191
00:05:56,312 --> 00:05:58,425
but still doesn't control all of them.

192
00:05:58,838 --> 00:06:00,138
After four years and,

193
00:06:00,375 --> 00:06:01,850
by the most conservative count,

194
00:06:02,125 --> 00:06:04,213
at least two hundred and fifty thousand Russian

195
00:06:04,225 --> 00:06:04,463
dead,

196
00:06:04,838 --> 00:06:06,975
he says Putin has gained only about one

197
00:06:06,988 --> 00:06:08,312
percent more of Ukraine.

198
00:06:08,725 --> 00:06:10,625
So Putin doesn't want a photo op with

199
00:06:10,638 --> 00:06:11,000
Trump.

200
00:06:11,425 --> 00:06:13,300
He wants Trump to deliver territory.

201
00:06:13,725 --> 00:06:15,213
And Pape doesn't think Trump can.

202
00:06:15,625 --> 00:06:17,050
Only Ukraine can give it up,

203
00:06:17,463 --> 00:06:19,488
and he believes Zelensky would be risking his

204
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:20,525
life if he did.

205
00:06:20,938 --> 00:06:22,100
So around Thanksgiving,

206
00:06:22,463 --> 00:06:24,650
Pape expects Putin to move to his next

207
00:06:24,662 --> 00:06:25,075
plan:

208
00:06:25,525 --> 00:06:26,275
a dark winter.

209
00:06:26,688 --> 00:06:28,600
Strike the power stations in Kyiv,

210
00:06:28,812 --> 00:06:30,300
Kharkiv and Odesa,

211
00:06:30,738 --> 00:06:32,562
aiming to knock out ninety percent of the

212
00:06:32,575 --> 00:06:34,088
power for a month or two.

213
00:06:34,512 --> 00:06:35,288
Because Putin,

214
00:06:35,662 --> 00:06:36,225
he says,

215
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:38,350
is in his own escalation trap.

216
00:06:38,762 --> 00:06:41,088
Accepting defeat could cost him not just his

217
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:42,625
legacy, but his life.

218
00:06:43,038 --> 00:06:44,700
Meanwhile, back in America,

219
00:06:44,950 --> 00:06:46,038
the third crisis:

220
00:06:46,338 --> 00:06:48,150
what Pape calls violent populism.

221
00:06:48,575 --> 00:06:51,200
That's when political violence moves from the fringe

222
00:06:51,212 --> 00:06:52,013
to the mainstream.

223
00:06:52,438 --> 00:06:54,175
He uses a wildfire analogy.

224
00:06:54,588 --> 00:06:55,450
In fire season,

225
00:06:55,712 --> 00:06:57,362
you can predict how much dry wood is

226
00:06:57,375 --> 00:06:58,138
lying around.

227
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:00,212
What you can't predict is the spark.

228
00:07:00,638 --> 00:07:02,700
The dry wood is the tens of millions

229
00:07:02,712 --> 00:07:05,162
of Americans who support political violence.

230
00:07:05,588 --> 00:07:07,038
The spark can be anything:

231
00:07:07,462 --> 00:07:08,425
a single post,

232
00:07:08,625 --> 00:07:11,375
a clash between federal agents and protesters over

233
00:07:11,388 --> 00:07:12,138
ballot counting.

234
00:07:12,550 --> 00:07:14,262
And then the crises start to feed each

235
00:07:14,275 --> 00:07:14,588
other.

236
00:07:15,013 --> 00:07:17,162
Pape points to riots in France that spread

237
00:07:17,175 --> 00:07:18,038
to other cities,

238
00:07:18,263 --> 00:07:19,612
and then beyond France.

239
00:07:20,025 --> 00:07:20,812
A big part of it,

240
00:07:21,013 --> 00:07:23,475
he says, was rising prices from the Iran

241
00:07:23,488 --> 00:07:23,825
war.

242
00:07:24,238 --> 00:07:25,938
Higher gas, higher diesel,

243
00:07:26,325 --> 00:07:27,675
a frozen housing market.

244
00:07:28,125 --> 00:07:30,025
Three crises, no longer separate.

245
00:07:30,546 --> 00:07:31,083
Part five.

246
00:07:31,496 --> 00:07:33,033
Troops, cyber attacks,

247
00:07:33,221 --> 00:07:34,196
and world war three.

248
00:07:34,608 --> 00:07:36,471
In Britain, as the host points out,

249
00:07:36,746 --> 00:07:39,283
the government has launched a Be Ready campaign.

250
00:07:39,696 --> 00:07:42,046
Every household is told to keep three days

251
00:07:42,058 --> 00:07:43,583
of supplies: water,

252
00:07:43,846 --> 00:07:44,683
tinned food,

253
00:07:45,096 --> 00:07:46,108
a battery radio,

254
00:07:46,346 --> 00:07:47,446
and physical cash,

255
00:07:47,683 --> 00:07:49,496
because ATMs may not work.

256
00:07:49,908 --> 00:07:50,183
Why?

257
00:07:50,596 --> 00:07:51,421
Cyber attacks.

258
00:07:51,846 --> 00:07:53,971
Pape points to a summer outage in Minnesota

259
00:07:54,121 --> 00:07:56,071
where power and water went down.

260
00:07:56,496 --> 00:07:58,483
He says a New York Times report quoted

261
00:07:58,496 --> 00:08:01,533
US intelligence saying it had Iran's fingerprints.

262
00:08:01,946 --> 00:08:03,496
And he finds it telling that the story

263
00:08:03,508 --> 00:08:04,633
was never followed up.

264
00:08:05,046 --> 00:08:05,933
Next on his board:

265
00:08:06,246 --> 00:08:07,708
troops in American streets.

266
00:08:08,133 --> 00:08:10,471
Pape says the president has refused to rule

267
00:08:10,483 --> 00:08:12,458
out invoking the Insurrection Act,

268
00:08:12,746 --> 00:08:14,883
which would let him use the military inside

269
00:08:14,896 --> 00:08:16,871
the country against its own citizens.

270
00:08:17,283 --> 00:08:20,596
Normally a law called posse comitatus prevents exactly

271
00:08:20,608 --> 00:08:22,696
that, because, as Pape puts it,

272
00:08:23,108 --> 00:08:24,808
that's what authoritarian states do.

273
00:08:25,246 --> 00:08:26,021
Back in Iran,

274
00:08:26,383 --> 00:08:28,321
he expects the bombing to leave a weakened

275
00:08:28,408 --> 00:08:30,146
but still dangerous military.

276
00:08:30,571 --> 00:08:32,246
That gives a reason to send in ground

277
00:08:32,258 --> 00:08:34,896
troops, like the Marines already moving,

278
00:08:35,183 --> 00:08:36,458
to hunt down the remnants.

279
00:08:36,871 --> 00:08:38,021
And once you go that far,

280
00:08:38,383 --> 00:08:39,071
Pape says,

281
00:08:39,471 --> 00:08:40,671
you're in a forever war.

282
00:08:41,083 --> 00:08:42,258
A real emergency,

283
00:08:42,483 --> 00:08:43,921
more emergency powers,

